WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.714
Often you do not receive

2
00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:06.030
entries one at a time.

3
00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:08.310
You have them all, in an array

4
00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:09.700
and you want a heap.

5
00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:13.260
The obvious method: insert them one by one.

6
00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:15.940
Each insert costs up to log n

7
00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:18.890
so that is up to n log n in total.

8
00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:22.358
Can we do better, by using the fact that we

9
00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:23.970
already have everything?

10
00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:25.310
We can.

11
00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:29.030
And the answer is surprising: n steps

12
00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:30.440
not n log n.

13
00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:32.525
In this video we'll see

14
00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:35.630
the bottom-up method, a worked example

15
00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:39.380
and why it adds up to only about n steps.

16
00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:41.930
Draw the array as a tree.

17
00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:43.930
It is not a heap yet.

18
00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:45.710
But look at the leaves

19
00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:47.021
the entries at the bottom.

20
00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:49.124
A single entry with no children

21
00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:51.000
is already a heap.

22
00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:53.440
So work from the bottom up.

23
00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:55.440
Take the last node that has a child

24
00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:56.460
and sink it

25
00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:58.830
exactly like the down-heap from last time.

26
00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:02.741
Then go back one index, and do it again.

27
00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:05.727
At each step the two subtrees below

28
00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:07.483
a node are already heaps

29
00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:09.406
so sinking the node makes the

30
00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:11.060
whole subtree a heap.

31
00:01:11.410 --> 00:01:13.340
When we have sunk the root

32
00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:15.370
the whole tree is a heap.

33
00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:18.800
Which is the last node that has a child?

34
00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:21.680
It is the parent of the last entry.

35
00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:23.620
Here there are ten entries

36
00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:25.200
the last index is nine

37
00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.430
and its parent is index four.

38
00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:32.410
So we sink the nodes at index four, three

39
00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:36.060
two, one, and zero, in that order.

40
00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:41.650
Our entries: twenty, sixteen, nine, twelve

41
00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:48.000
five, eight, three, fourteen, six, eleven.

42
00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:52.160
Index four holds five, with one child

43
00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:53.260
eleven.

44
00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:55.320
Five is smaller, so nothing to do.

45
00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:56.790
One comparison.

46
00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:59.430
Index three holds twelve

47
00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:02.030
with children fourteen and six.

48
00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:03.610
Six is the smaller child

49
00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:05.630
and smaller than twelve, so swap.

50
00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:08.140
Two comparisons, one swap.

51
00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:10.520
Index two holds nine

52
00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:12.520
with children eight and three.

53
00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:13.600
Three is smaller.

54
00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:14.340
Swap.

55
00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:16.690
Two comparisons, one swap.

56
00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.550
Index one holds sixteen

57
00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:21.770
with children six and five.

58
00:02:21.770 --> 00:02:23.690
Five is smaller: swap

59
00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:26.750
and then it sinks again, past eleven.

60
00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:29.040
Three comparisons, two swaps.

61
00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:32.360
Finally index zero holds twenty.

62
00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:33.980
It sinks through three

63
00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:36.200
and then through eight, to the bottom.

64
00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:38.630
Four comparisons, two swaps.

65
00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:41.150
The result is a valid heap.

66
00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:45.140
In total: twelve comparisons, and six swaps.

67
00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:47.340
Pause and predict.

68
00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:48.876
Insert the same ten entries

69
00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:50.830
one at a time instead.

70
00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:52.330
Do you get the same heap?

71
00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:53.640
Do you pay the same?

72
00:02:53.990 --> 00:02:55.860
Not the same heap.

73
00:02:55.860 --> 00:03:00.380
Inserting one by one gives three, six, five

74
00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:04.600
nine, eleven, sixteen, eight, twenty

75
00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:05.860
fourteen, twelve.

76
00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:09.070
A different array, and equally valid.

77
00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:11.370
And not the same price

78
00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:14.170
seventeen comparisons and twelve swaps

79
00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:16.300
against twelve and six.

80
00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:18.860
A heap is not unique.

81
00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:21.080
Many arrays satisfy the order rule

82
00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:23.250
and the two methods pick different ones.

83
00:03:23.830 --> 00:03:25.700
Why is bottom-up cheap?

84
00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:28.780
Look at how much work each node can cost.

85
00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:31.380
Sinking a node costs at most its height

86
00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:33.350
the number of levels below it.

87
00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:36.390
And most of the nodes are near the bottom.

88
00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:38.670
About half of all the nodes are leaves

89
00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:39.790
with height zero.

90
00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:41.060
They cost nothing.

91
00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:43.546
About a quarter are one level

92
00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:44.500
above the leaves.

93
00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:47.020
They cost at most one level each.

94
00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:49.940
About an eighth are two levels above

95
00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:51.530
and cost at most two.

96
00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:54.650
Add it up: n over four times one

97
00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:56.770
plus n over eight times two

98
00:03:56.770 --> 00:04:00.030
plus n over sixteen times three, and so on.

99
00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:02.720
This sum is smaller than n.

100
00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:05.780
Only the single root costs the full height

101
00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:06.600
log n.

102
00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:08.150
There is one of it.

103
00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:11.240
Compare with inserting one by one.

104
00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:13.385
There, it is the entries at the bottom

105
00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:14.640
that are expensive

106
00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:17.330
a new entry may climb all the way up.

107
00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:19.175
The two methods spend their

108
00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:21.310
effort in opposite places.

109
00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:24.090
Bottom-up makes the many cheap

110
00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:25.830
and the few expensive.

111
00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:27.330
Inserting does the opposite

112
00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:28.560
in the worst case.

113
00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:30.420
For decreasing keys

114
00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:32.651
with a thousand entries

115
00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:34.856
bottom-up needs one thousand nine

116
00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:36.858
hundred eighty-two comparisons.

117
00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:39.966
Inserting one by one needs seven thousand

118
00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:41.790
nine hundred eighty-seven.

119
00:04:42.140 --> 00:04:44.440
With sixty-four thousand entries

120
00:04:44.510 --> 00:04:45.760
bottom-up needs a hundred

121
00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:46.890
twenty-eight thousand.

122
00:04:46.890 --> 00:04:49.406
Almost exactly two per entry.

123
00:04:49.490 --> 00:04:51.249
Inserting needs eight hundred

124
00:04:51.310 --> 00:04:52.730
ninety-four thousand

125
00:04:52.730 --> 00:04:54.860
about fourteen per entry.

126
00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:57.666
One honest caveat.

127
00:04:57.790 --> 00:04:59.627
Decreasing keys are the worst

128
00:04:59.690 --> 00:05:00.930
case for inserting.

129
00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:03.844
With random keys, a new entry usually stops

130
00:05:03.910 --> 00:05:05.285
after a level or two

131
00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:08.181
and inserting one by one costs only about

132
00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:10.720
two point three comparisons per entry.

133
00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:12.980
Bottom-up still wins

134
00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:14.900
with about one point nine

135
00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:16.620
and it has a guarantee

136
00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:19.300
never more than two comparisons per entry

137
00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:20.340
whatever the input.

138
00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:23.920
The n log n for inserting is a worst case

139
00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:25.070
not an average.

140
00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:26.680
To sum up.

141
00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:29.500
To build a heap from n entries

142
00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:31.840
sink every node that has a child

143
00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:34.130
from the last one back to the root.

144
00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:36.610
Most nodes are near the bottom

145
00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:37.935
where sinking is cheap

146
00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:39.691
so the total is at most about

147
00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:41.640
two n comparisons.

148
00:05:41.990 --> 00:05:44.920
Next: a surprising use of this.

149
00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:47.780
Build a heap, then take everything out.

150
00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:49.720
That sorts the array in place.

151
00:05:49.720 --> 00:05:51.310
It is called heap sort.

152
00:05:51.937 --> 00:05:53.097
Let's build a heap from

153
00:05:53.147 --> 00:05:56.097
a collection in Java, and count the steps.

154
00:05:56.677 --> 00:05:58.287
The method is three lines.

155
00:05:58.287 --> 00:06:01.107
We loop j from the parent of the last entry

156
00:06:01.107 --> 00:06:04.517
down to zero, and call down-heap on each.

157
00:06:04.867 --> 00:06:06.690
The constructor copies the

158
00:06:06.777 --> 00:06:10.067
collection you pass in, then calls heapify.

159
00:06:10.417 --> 00:06:13.578
Our class also keeps a second constructor that

160
00:06:13.647 --> 00:06:16.817
takes only a comparator and starts empty.

161
00:06:17.397 --> 00:06:20.647
Here is the real run on our ten entries.

162
00:06:20.647 --> 00:06:24.307
Fix node four: one comparison, no swap.

163
00:06:24.307 --> 00:06:27.387
Node three: two comparisons, one swap.

164
00:06:27.387 --> 00:06:29.607
Node two: two and one.

165
00:06:29.607 --> 00:06:31.807
Node one: three and two.

166
00:06:31.807 --> 00:06:33.717
Node zero: four and two.

167
00:06:34.067 --> 00:06:37.437
Total: twelve comparisons and six swaps.

168
00:06:37.437 --> 00:06:39.747
And the result is a valid heap.

169
00:06:40.327 --> 00:06:43.457
The same ten entries inserted one by one

170
00:06:43.457 --> 00:06:46.377
seventeen comparisons and twelve swaps.

171
00:06:46.377 --> 00:06:48.927
A different array, equally valid.

172
00:06:49.507 --> 00:06:51.217
Now let's measure at scale.

173
00:06:51.217 --> 00:06:54.289
For n entries in decreasing order

174
00:06:54.377 --> 00:06:56.113
we count the comparisons of

175
00:06:56.177 --> 00:06:57.597
bottom-up construction

176
00:06:57.597 --> 00:06:59.867
and of n separate inserts.

177
00:07:00.217 --> 00:07:02.367
At a thousand entries

178
00:07:02.367 --> 00:07:04.394
one thousand nine hundred eighty-two

179
00:07:04.447 --> 00:07:05.951
against seven thousand nine

180
00:07:06.007 --> 00:07:07.147
hundred eighty-seven.

181
00:07:07.147 --> 00:07:09.367
At sixty-four thousand

182
00:07:09.367 --> 00:07:10.987
a hundred twenty-eight thousand

183
00:07:10.987 --> 00:07:14.057
against almost nine hundred thousand.

184
00:07:14.407 --> 00:07:17.497
The first column stays near two per entry.

185
00:07:17.497 --> 00:07:21.527
The second keeps growing, like log n.

186
00:07:22.107 --> 00:07:25.070
With random order, the gap closes.

187
00:07:25.157 --> 00:07:27.269
Bottom-up costs about one point

188
00:07:27.337 --> 00:07:29.157
nine comparisons per entry

189
00:07:29.157 --> 00:07:31.297
and inserting about two point three.

190
00:07:31.297 --> 00:07:33.157
Bottom-up is still cheaper

191
00:07:33.157 --> 00:07:36.387
and its guarantee holds for every input.

192
00:07:36.967 --> 00:07:39.278
Java's Priority Queue has a constructor

193
00:07:39.337 --> 00:07:40.937
that takes a collection

194
00:07:40.937 --> 00:07:43.397
and it uses this very method

195
00:07:43.397 --> 00:07:44.727
in linear time.

196
00:07:45.077 --> 00:07:47.843
Our version produces the same array as that

197
00:07:47.907 --> 00:07:49.627
constructor for distinct entries

198
00:07:49.627 --> 00:07:51.237
the test suite checks it.

199
00:07:51.587 --> 00:07:53.008
But beware

200
00:07:53.137 --> 00:07:55.875
creating an empty Priority Queue and calling

201
00:07:55.937 --> 00:07:58.897
addAll adds the entries one at a time

202
00:07:58.897 --> 00:08:00.397
the n log n way.

203
00:08:00.397 --> 00:08:02.657
If you already have the collection

204
00:08:02.657 --> 00:08:04.227
pass it to the constructor.

205
00:08:04.807 --> 00:08:06.817
Two common mistakes.

206
00:08:06.817 --> 00:08:10.097
One: looping upward instead of downward.

207
00:08:10.097 --> 00:08:12.817
Sinking the root first does not work

208
00:08:12.817 --> 00:08:15.567
because its subtrees are not heaps yet.

209
00:08:15.917 --> 00:08:18.581
Two: starting at the last index

210
00:08:18.667 --> 00:08:21.600
instead of its parent, which is wasted work

211
00:08:21.667 --> 00:08:23.815
and breaks if you do not check

212
00:08:23.887 --> 00:08:25.317
that children exist.

213
00:08:25.897 --> 00:08:29.286
So in Java: heapify from the last internal

214
00:08:29.367 --> 00:08:30.425
node to the root

215
00:08:30.487 --> 00:08:32.208
or pass the collection to the

216
00:08:32.267 --> 00:08:33.787
Priority Queue constructor.

217
00:08:33.787 --> 00:08:36.377
Next: heap sort.

